// `Hash` end-to-end (ADR 0005 §2, Phase 2.2a Task 6).
//
// Three things this pins that no type-level test can. That every primitive
// `Hash` impl actually runs. That `Hash for Char` recovers the codepoint —
// its `char_to_int` builtin lowers to a register move, so a wrong temp would
// still type-check and only a run would notice. And that the **low** bits of
// an `Int` hash are spread, because `Map` selects buckets with
// `hash & (cap - 1)`: a weakly-mixed hash is not merely ugly there, it
// collapses whole key families onto one bucket.
//
// SEEDED, WHICH IS WHY SOME LINES BELOW ARE BOUNDS RATHER THAN VALUES.
// `std/core`'s `Int`, `Bool` and `Char` impls XOR a per-process seed into
// `mix64`'s input, so any statistic over a fixed key set is a random variable
// unless the seed cancels. Each `Int`, `Bool` and `Char` line here is either
// seed-invariant by construction or a bound whose false-failure probability is
// derived below. The two `String` lines are a third case: `str_hash` carries its
// own per-process seed and seeded FNV-1a with a finalizer is not injective, so
// their `diff=` halves are seed-dependent at a magnitude around 2^-64 — far
// below every figure below and left unbounded on purpose.
//
// Summing the bounds below puts this fixture's whole flake budget at about one
// run in a billion, PER PROCESS OF THIS FIXTURE. One significant figure is all
// the argument needs, and the total is deliberately not carried further: the
// summands are published at three, three and seven figures, so adding up what
// is printed beside them cannot reproduce a total stated to ten — it diverges
// from the true sum in the fifth figure. For the suite-level rate, note that
// `hash.stdout` is compared by two tests, `hash_run` and
// `hash_build_standalone`, each running this fixture in its own process, so the
// rate per suite run is about twice the per-process one. And the sum is a
// Bonferroni bound WITHIN THE MODEL described
// next, not a bound on the run: the summands are not disjoint events, and the
// model is not the true joint distribution.
//
// A NOTE ON HOW NOT TO CHECK THIS FILE. Standing a literal `0` in for the seed
// call reproduces the unseeded output by construction, since `x ^ 0 == x`, so
// such a probe cannot tell "invariant over every seed" from "identical at seed
// 0". Two lines were misclassified that way while this split was being planned.
// Reason about whether the seed cancels, or sample many seeds.
//
// THE DERIVED BOUNDS, computed rather than sampled.
//
// WHAT "COMPUTED" BUYS AND WHAT IT DOES NOT, because "exact" alone would
// overstate it. Each figure below comes from a finite exact summation over
// rationals — inclusion-exclusion across the occupancy distribution, or a
// binomial tail — rather than from a simulation, so no sampling error enters,
// and none is a closed form. What does enter is
// a model: each figure is exact for a model in which the hashes involved are
// independent and uniform. They are not independent. Every one of them is a
// deterministic function of a single 64-bit seed, so at most 2^64 outcomes stand
// where the model for the 128-key line alone has 256^128, and the model
// therefore cannot be the true joint distribution. What the model describes is
// the mixer a good finalizer is meant to approximate, which is the thing these
// lines exist to check — so read each figure as exact for the model and as an
// estimate for the run. Any figure that is additionally a sum over non-disjoint
// events is an upper bound within that model, and is labelled Bonferroni: the
// flake budget above, and the rejected largest-bucket figure below.
//
//   * `keys -64..63 reach >= 76 of 256 buckets`. 128 keys into 256 buckets:
//     `P(exactly j occupied) = C(256,j) * Surj(128,j) / 256^128`, whose
//     probabilities sum to 1.000000 and whose expectation is 100.88.
//     `P(distinct < 76)` is 2.37e-11, about one run in 42 billion. 20,000
//     simulated seeds gave a minimum of 85, nine above the bound.
//   * `Int hashes of 0..255 that are negative in 80..176`. Under a uniform seed
//     the sign bit over a fixed key is a fair coin, so the count is
//     `Binomial(256, 1/2)` with mean 128, and outside 80..176 is 8.41e-10,
//     about one run in 1.19 billion. The observed range over 1000 seeds was
//     100 to 153.
//   * `buckets reached >= 6`. 64 keys into 8 buckets, exact occupancy again:
//     `P(used < 6)` is 4.836243e-12, about one run in 207 billion.
//
// WHY `buckets reached` IS A BOUND RATHER THAN `8 of 8`. Unseeded that count
// was a constant and the constant was 8. Seeded it is a random variable, and
// `P(some bucket empty)` is 1.554270e-03 — about one run in 643, nine and a
// half times worse than the largest-bucket bound this file rejects by name just
// below. `used >= 7` would be 2.825296e-07, one run in 3,539,451, still a
// factor of 336 looser than the loosest bound here — two and a half orders;
// `used >= 6` sits in the same band as the bounds listed above, so 6 is the
// bound. Nothing real is lost: what the line guards is that hashing is not the
// identity, and `fn hash(self) -> Int { self }` puts all 64 multiples of 8 in
// bucket 0, reaching 1. A constant hash also reaches 1.
//
// WHY THE TWO `&7` HISTOGRAMS ARE GONE RATHER THAN REPLACED. They pinned exact
// bucket counts, which the seed randomises. The obvious replacement is a
// largest-bucket bound, as `tests/runtime/hash_diffusion.nova` uses, and it is
// a trap: for 256 keys in 8 buckets that count is `Binomial(256, 1/8)` with
// mean 32, and the per-bucket tail summed over the buckets puts even
// `largest < 56` at 1.62e-04 — a Bonferroni upper bound, since the per-bucket
// events are not disjoint, so about one run in 6,169 at worst. That is the same
// order as the undisclosed flake the previous increment had to remove, which
// fired about one run in 6,100. Eight buckets is too few for a tight bound at
// this key count. What the histograms guarded is that hashing is not the
// identity, and `buckets reached >= 6` asserts that directly.
//
// THE LOW-BIT COVERAGE THAT LEAVES, since both histograms and the loosening of
// `buckets reached` land together. Nothing in this file constrains the 8-bucket
// DISTRIBUTION any more — only its support. `buckets reached >= 6` still masks
// three low bits over 64 keys and still asserts they do not collapse onto a
// handful of buckets, but it says nothing about how evenly they spread, which
// is the part the histograms pinned. So the `mcounts` loop behind that line is
// LIVE and must stay; what went for want of a consumer is the separate `counts`
// loop that fed the deleted `keys 0..255 &7` histogram. What covers low bits
// instead is stronger, not weaker: `keys -64..63 reach >= 76 of 256` masks
// eight low bits over 128 keys spanning both signs, where the histograms
// sampled three bits over one sign.
//
// `complement collisions over 0..63: 0` IS NOW A THEOREM, NOT A MEASUREMENT.
// The pairs are `x` and `-1-x`; `mix64` is injective; and
// `mix64(x ^ s) == mix64((-1-x) ^ s)` would need `x ^ s == (-1-x) ^ s`, hence
// `x == -1-x`, which no `Int` satisfies. So this line reads 0 at every seed
// rather than at the seeds someone sampled.

fn hash_of<T: Hash>(x: T) -> Int { x.hash() }

// The per-process seed, recovered so the canonical vectors can cancel it.
//
// `(0).hash()` is `mix64(0 ^ s)`, which is `mix64(s)`, and `mix64` is a
// bijection — so one call plus this inverse yields `s` exactly. That recovery
// is a documented consequence of ADR 0005's one-shot `Hash` returning a plain
// `Int`, not a hole this fixture opens: the same disclosure already records
// that `("").hash()` recovers the string seed. Nothing exercised either
// channel, so this fixture demonstrates the `Int` one rather than leaving it
// asserted.
//
// Why the inverse is written out here instead of calling `mix64`: ADR 0005
// makes `mix64` module-private to `std/core` on purpose — an implementation
// detail of the primitive impls rather than a published utility, since
// publishing it would make its exact definition a compatibility surface — and
// the same ADR reserves the freedom to replace its constants or rounds. A
// fixture cannot reach a non-`pub` std item, and adding `pub` to reach it would
// spend that freedom.
//
// THE MAINTENANCE COST, named because it is the real one: if `mix64`'s
// constants ever change, this inverse changes with them. The two multiplies are
// by the modular inverses of `mix64`'s constants mod 2^64 —
// `0x96de1b173f119089` and `0x319642b2d24d8ec3`, written as signed decimals for
// the same reason `mix64`'s own constants are. Each stage `z ^ lsr(z, k)`
// inverts as `z ^ lsr(z, k) ^ lsr(z, 2k) ^ …` until the shift reaches 64, and
// because Nova's `>>` is arithmetic every `lsr(x, k)` is written
// `(x >> k) & (2^(64-k) - 1)` — which is where the six mask constants come
// from, three of them already appearing in `mix64` itself.
fn mix64_inv(z: Int) -> Int {
    let d = z ^ ((z >> 31) & 8589934591) ^ ((z >> 62) & 3)
    let c = d * 3573116690164977347
    let b = c ^ ((c >> 27) & 137438953471) ^ ((c >> 54) & 1023)
    let a = b * -7575587736534282103
    a ^ ((a >> 30) & 17179869183) ^ ((a >> 60) & 15)
}

fn main() {
    // Deterministic within a run, and distinguishing, for each of the four
    // impls. Deterministic across runs for none of them: the seed is drawn
    // once per process, so `same=` compares two calls under one seed.
    println("Int    same=${(7).hash() == (7).hash()} diff=${(7).hash() != (8).hash()}")
    println("Bool   same=${(true).hash() == (true).hash()} diff=${(true).hash() != (false).hash()}")
    println("Char   same=${('a').hash() == ('a').hash()} diff=${('a').hash() != ('b').hash()}")
    println("String same=${("hello").hash() == ("hello").hash()} diff=${("hello").hash() != ("world").hash()}")
    println("String empty=${("").hash() == ("").hash()} diff=${("").hash() != ("a").hash()}")

    // Reached through a `T: Hash` bound — the shape `Map`/`Set` will use.
    println("bound  ${hash_of(7) == (7).hash()} ${hash_of('a') == ('a').hash()} ${hash_of("hello") == ("hello").hash()}")

    // `Hash for Char` hashes the codepoint, so it agrees with `Hash for Int`
    // on that codepoint ('a' is 97). This is what proves `char_to_int`
    // yielded the codepoint rather than an unrelated register. Both impls XOR
    // the same seed into the same input, so the agreement is exact at every
    // seed — and seeding one impl and not the other breaks this line.
    println("char/int agree ${('a').hash() == (97).hash()} ${('\0').hash() == (0).hash()}")

    // The case identity hashing gets catastrophically wrong: 64 keys that are
    // all multiples of 8. `fn hash(self) -> Int { self }` puts every one of
    // them in bucket 0 of an 8-bucket table, and a constant hash does too.
    // The bound is 6 of 8 for the reason derived in the header.
    let mut mcounts = [0; 8]
    let mut used = 0
    let mut k = 0
    while k < 64 {
        let b = (k * 8).hash() & 7
        if mcounts[b] == 0 { used = used + 1 }
        mcounts[b] = mcounts[b] + 1
        k = k + 1
    }
    println("buckets reached >= 6: ${used >= 6} (identity hashing would reach 1)")

    // CANONICAL VECTORS. `mix64` is the splitmix64 finalizer, which has
    // published test vectors: splitmix64 seeded at 0 emits the finalizer
    // applied to successive multiples of the gamma constant
    // 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15. Two of those pairs pin all three of `mix64`'s shift
    // masks at once — and after the golden split they are the only pin left on
    // the second mask, see the scope note on the complement pairs below.
    //
    //   x1 = 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15 -> 0xE220A8397B1DCDAF   pins masks 1 and 3
    //   x3 = 0xDAA66D2C7DDF743F -> 0x06C45D188009454F   pins masks 1 and 2
    //
    // Each is blind to one mask because the shift that mask covers happens not
    // to sign-extend for that input (x1's stage-2 input is non-negative, x3's
    // stage-3 input is), so both are needed. Written as signed decimals
    // because `Int` has no unsigned literals and the lexer's hex path parses
    // as i64, so `0x9e37…` does not lex.
    //
    // They can no longer be asserted as `(x1).hash()`: that route XORs the
    // seed in, and `mix64(x1 ^ s)` is not the canonical output for `x1` at any
    // seed but zero. XORing the recovered seed into the input cancels it
    // exactly — `.hash()` is `mix64(k ^ s)` and `(x1 ^ s) ^ s == x1` — so both
    // halves print `true` at every seed. Verified over 20,000 seeds while this
    // was designed, and the whole point of `mix64_inv` above.
    let s = mix64_inv((0).hash())
    println("splitmix64 canonical x1=${((-7046029254386353131) ^ s).hash() == -2152535657050944081} x3=${((-2691343689449507777) ^ s).hash() == 487617019471545679}")

    // COMPLEMENT PAIRS. This is the regression that matters most, and the one
    // every bucket-spread line above is blind to because they all sample a
    // single sign. Nova's `>>` is arithmetic, and arithmetic shift commutes
    // with complement, so an unmasked `x ^ (x >> k)` returns the same value for
    // `x` and for `!x == -1 - x`. XOR with a constant seed commutes with
    // complement too — `(!k) ^ s == !(k ^ s)` — so seeding does not rescue an
    // unmasked stage 1: every later stage is a function of its output, and an
    // unmasked mix64 folds 2-to-1 with identical hashes, meaning each pair
    // shares a bucket at every capacity and no resize can separate them.
    //
    // Scope, because this is easy to overstate: the two lines below pin the
    // FIRST mask, `(x >> 30) & 17179869183` — the only one whose absence makes
    // stage 1 itself 2-to-1 under `x <-> !x`. They are blind to the other two.
    // What they are not is statistical. Both are theorems and hold at EVERY
    // seed, not at almost every seed: with mask 1 gone the count below reads 64
    // against a golden of 0 and `h(0) != h(-1)` reads false, whatever the seed
    // is. That makes them mask 1's strongest pin, ahead of the `keys -64..63`
    // bound, which catches mask 1 too but as a bound. Measured for all three
    // masks from the object-file side; see `hash_build_standalone`'s roster.
    // Masking a shift by `k` alters only bits at positions >= 64 - k, and a
    // multiply carries low bits upward, never high bits down, so dropping the
    // third mask cannot move `hash & 255` at all; dropping the second leaves
    // stage 1 intact, so complement pairs no longer collide.
    //
    // WHAT CATCHES THOSE TWO CHANGED WITH THE GOLDEN SPLIT, and the change is
    // a loss on one of them. Before the seed reached these impls, the
    // both-signs bucket count was an exact 100 that moved to 107 without the
    // second mask, and the negative-hash count an exact 132 that moved to 0
    // without the third; both are bounds now. Mask 3 keeps a second pin, since
    // 0 falls outside `80..176`. Mask 2 does not: 107 sits comfortably inside
    // `>= 76`, so the bucket count no longer pins it, and no looser statistic
    // recovers it either — a mixer missing mask 2 still reaches 107 of 256
    // buckets, which no bound wide enough to avoid flaking can distinguish
    // from a healthy 100. Mask 2 now rests on the canonical vectors alone,
    // which is why they are still asserted here rather than dropped.
    println("h(0) != h(-1): ${(0).hash() != (-1).hash()}")
    let mut folded = 0
    let mut p = 0
    while p < 64 {
        if (p).hash() == (-1 - p).hash() { folded = folded + 1 }
        p = p + 1
    }
    println("complement collisions over 0..63: ${folded}")

    // Keys spanning BOTH signs, over 256 buckets. 128 distinct keys into 256
    // buckets uniformly is ~101 distinct buckets; the unmasked mixer reached
    // only 58, because 64 of the 128 keys were complement pairs of the others.
    // The bound is 76 for the reason derived in the header, and it is
    // load-bearing rather than decorative: an unmasked stage 1 leaves at most
    // 64 distinct hashes over these 128 keys, so it fails this line too.
    let mut seen = [0; 256]
    let mut distinct = 0
    let mut q = -64
    while q < 64 {
        let b = (q).hash() & 255
        if seen[b] == 0 { distinct = distinct + 1 }
        seen[b] = 1
        q = q + 1
    }
    println("keys -64..63 reach >= 76 of 256 buckets: ${distinct >= 76} (unmasked reached 58)")

    // A hash spans the full Int range, both signs — so `hash % cap` could
    // produce a negative index and `hash >> k` is not a usable tag. Bucket
    // selection must mask; see the rule on `Hash` in std/core.
    let mut neg = 0
    let mut n = 0
    while n < 256 {
        if (n).hash() < 0 { neg = neg + 1 }
        n = n + 1
    }
    println("Int hashes of 0..255 that are negative in 80..176: ${(neg >= 80) && (neg <= 176)} (masking is mandatory)")
}
